-- S10 -- F1'S `refcount -= 1` -- «B29», AND «B31» BESIDE IT.
--
-- «B31» said: *"`old(place)` hangs under `atompred`, not under `primary` -- it
-- can stand as a predicate on its own, but cannot occur in any expression. NO
-- DIFFERENCE STATEMENT ("after against before") IS WRITABLE."* -- **CLOSED.**
-- The `ensures` below writes `Objekte.slots[o].refs == old(Objekte.slots[o].refs)
-- - 1`, i.e. `old` inside an arithmetic expression, and it is accepted.
-- (`beispiele/104`:38 carries the same shape.)
--
-- «B29» is the one that stands: *"Relational precondition: `refcount -= 1`
-- falls only via an invariant that after B13 is not writable at all. The
-- contested case of the dividing line, on a real case."*
--
-- MEASURED. The body below is `Objekte.slots[o].refs -= 1;` under
-- `requires Objekte.slots[o].refs >= 1`, and the file is REFUSED:
--
--   error: [M104] ... `Objekte.slots[…].refs` -= leaves the range:
--                 `u32 in 0 .. 64` against `u8 in 1 .. 1`
--   error: [M101] ... the assignment requires `u32 in 0 .. 64`,
--                 the value has `u32 in -1 .. 63`
--
-- The `requires` is not read by the range pass. Two shapes DO pass, and both
-- cost the same thing:
--
--   if Objekte.slots[o].refs >= 1 { Objekte.slots[o].refs -= 1; }     0 errors
--   narrow Objekte.slots[o].refs to 1 .. 64 else { return; }          0 errors
--
-- i.e. the precondition is written twice, once as a promise the caller owes
-- and once as a branch the callee runs. `M104`'s own note says so:
-- *"a check before it narrows the range (V1), a relation between two places
-- carries too (V2)"* -- a RELATION between two PLACES carries; a relation
-- between a place and a CONSTANT, in the `requires`, does not.

module schreibprobe::refcount {

const NOBJ : u32 = 16;

type Refs = u32 in 0 .. 64;

table Objekte count NOBJ {
    slot {
        refs : Refs,
    }
}

impl fn freigeben(o : index into Objekte)
    requires Objekte.slots[o].refs >= 1
    ensures  Objekte.slots[o].refs == old(Objekte.slots[o].refs) - 1
    effects  { reads Objekte.slots, writes Objekte.slots }
    costs    <= 64 ops
{
    Objekte.slots[o].refs -= 1;
}

}
